import json,pathlib
lines=['# Literature record — G0006','', 'Search date: 2026-10-11. All web requests used Python urllib, not built-in web search.','', '## Verified source', '', 'John Fernley and Emmanuel Jacob, *Percolation of the contact process on the regular tree*, arXiv:2609.09972v1, posted 9 September 2026; PDF dated 10 September 2026, 19 pages. https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.09972', '', 'Definition on PDF pp. 1–2: nearest-neighbor graph Z^d, recovery rate 1, rate lambda along each infected–healthy edge. nu_lambda is the upper invariant law. lambda_1 is the survival critical value; lambda_p is the supremum of rates having zero probability of an infinite nearest-neighbor infected component. The relevant open-question paragraph is actually PDF p. 3, not pp. 1–2 in the intake locator.', '', 'Exact question paragraph on PDF p. 3 (typographical ligatures normalized):', '', '> The same paper also raises the natural question whether we have λ₁ < λₚ on Zᵈ when d ≥ 2, which remains a difficult open problem. To the best of our knowledge, such a result has previously never been proven for any graph with λₚ < +∞.', '', 'Theorem 2.1(a), PDF p. 3, says on the d-regular tree T_d with d >= 3, lambda_1 < lambda_p. This is NOT the lattice theorem. Tree degree d must not be confused with lattice dimension d (degree 2d).', '', 'Reference [8]: Thomas M. Liggett and Jeffrey E. Steif, *Stochastic domination: the contact process, Ising models and FKG measures*, Ann. Inst. H. Poincare Probab. Statist. 42(2) (2006), 223–243, arXiv:math/0504530. Downloaded original preprint. Theorem 1.1, preprint PDF p. 2, proves domination from BELOW by product measures for sufficiently LARGE rates. This yields percolation, not nonpercolation.', '', 'Reference [11]: Balazs Rath and Daniel Valesin, *On the threshold of spread-out contact process percolation*, Ann. Inst. Henri Poincare Probab. Stat. 58(3) (2022), 1808–1848, DOI 10.1214/21-AIHP1214, arXiv:1912.09825. Different infection graph; must not count as solving nearest-neighbor model.', '', '## Requests and outcomes','']
for fn in sorted(pathlib.Path('.').glob('search_log*.json')):
 for r in json.loads(fn.read_text(encoding='utf8')):
  lines += ['- '+r['service']+': `'+r['url']+'` — '+('HTTP '+str(r['status'])+', '+str(r.get('bytes','?'))+' bytes' if 'status' in r else r['error'])+'.']
lines+=['', 'OpenAlex source lookup and both searches were rate limited. Its citing-works query could not be completed because no OpenAlex source work ID could be resolved; the provisional arXiv-valued `cites` request is not a valid completed citation search. Semantic Scholar source citation request and title search both returned 429. These are coverage gaps, not evidence of no citations.', '', '## Initial search findings', '', 'arXiv post-source date search returned the tree source, generalized deterministic-cure processes, social clusters/asymptomatic models, machine-learning of the contact process, comparison of infection rules, k-creation processes, and quenched-disorder bootstrap work. None of the inspected abstracts states the requested nearest-neighbor upper-invariant strict inequality. GitHub returned one simulation repository, lorenzomancini1/SIS_and_SIR_model_percolation; not a proof.', '', 'Crossref broad search identified sharpness papers and spread-out work; these are leads requiring hypothesis inspection. No SOLVED_IN_LITERATURE verdict is warranted.', '', '## Read-only brain and earlier attempts', '', 'Ran math_recall.py with "contact process percolation Liggett Steif"; results were mostly off topic. Ran brain.py recall separately on "contact process upper invariant measure percolation mistakes" and "contact process upper invariant measure spatial percolation"; no trustworthy directly relevant proof surfaced.', '', 'Read G0006-A001 from probability_1000_intake_G_20260910/attempts.jsonl. It reverses a stochastic-domination direction and misdescribes the tree proof. Neither lower product domination below p_c nor failure to dominate a product law above p_c implies nonpercolation. Only an UPPER subcritical product comparison would do so. Source itself warns that arbitrarily low density alone is insufficient.', '', 'Searched codex_pool_2026_10_10 and origin/master tree paths for G0006/contact_process/contact.process; no matching paths. No repositories or brain data modified.']
pathlib.Path('LITERATURE.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n',encoding='utf8')
pathlib.Path('ATTEMPT.md').write_text('# G0006 — research in progress\n\nExact target: for every integer d >= 2, the nearest-neighbor contact process on Z^d with recovery rate 1 and per-edge infection rate lambda has lambda_c(d) < lambda_p(d), where percolation is nearest-neighbor site percolation in its upper invariant measure.\n\nThe source verifies this is open as of its September 2026 version. No proof has been found in the searches so far.\n\nRoutes being checked: stationarity of one- and two-site observables; product upper bounds via graphical races; finite-window survival and spatial renormalization. Continuity of density alone does not control dependent spatial connectivity.\n',encoding='utf8')
